			<div class="content" id="post-content-3864641">
									

<p>Reserved for author commentary and image credits.</p>
<p>Commentary: The theme of this article is, for me, a bit of site history. My very first post to the wiki was a SCP write-up of a reanimated zombie of James Garfield. It was terrible and didn't last more than a few hours before deletion (though a fragment did end up here: <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-100-j">http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-100-j</a>). I did vow, however, to return to the subject some day. The 4000 contest motivated me to actually follow through.</p>
<p>A word about &quot;I have been called many names&#8230;&quot; Yes, it's intended as corny and over-the-top. There are two kinds of period-fidelity that I'm going for in this piece: the first is text-based period-fidelity by which I mean conformity of the epistolary documentation to the style of the time. This is part of my method: if I'm going to write a fictional journal entry by Madame Blavatsky or a fictional letter by Alexander Graham Bell to his wife, first I read examples of authentic documents of that type so that I can try to capture the style and cadence of that sort of writing. The second is narrative period-fidelity. Think of your favorite late-19th-century genre writers: Bram Stoker; H.G. Wells; Jules Verne; Arthur Conan Doyle; Rudyard Kipling &#8230; there is in them a somewhat refreshingly shameless boldness - after all, the tropes they were using weren't yet cliches.</p>
<p>Basically everybody in the story is an actual historic character. Bell and his wife Mabel really did have a passing interest in spiritualism and the occult- then again, it seems like basically everybody did in that time period. Crookes was a Theosophist as well as a Royal Fellow. Lucretia Garfield really was a ghost-hunter. In our timeline, Dr. Bliss remained in charge of Garfield's care until Garfield's slow, painful death by infection, and there is no indication that Bell remained part of the team apart from the single-visit attempt to use his apparatus to find the bullet. Bell really did speak Mohawk, though, and was friends with George Johnson.</p>
<p>And Madame Blavatsky wasn't in the United States at the time of the Garfield assassination- she was in Asia. (The words that I put in her mouth are, however, largely hers. She was quite a cantankerous person.)</p>
<p>I didn't initially mean for this to be Yet Another Foundation Origin Story, but there you have it.</p>
<p>Some image links:</p>
<ul>
<li>Everything except for the Visible Speech excerpt is from Wikimedia Commons</li>
<li>The Visible Speech excerpt is from Bell's actual letter to his wife in that script, from the Library of Congress collection: <a href="https://www.loc.gov/item/magbell.03500108/">https://www.loc.gov/item/magbell.03500108/</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Some text credits:</p>
<ul>
<li>Helena Blavatsky's cranky letters to the editor, and the style (but not the content) of her diary entries, comes from <a href="http://www.katinkahesselink.net/blavatsky/">http://www.katinkahesselink.net/blavatsky/</a></li>
<li>Specific cited quotations from Blavatsky's published writings come from the cited sources</li>
<li>The New York Times excerpt is word-for-word from <a href="https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1881/07/03/issue.html">https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1881/07/03/issue.html</a></li>
</ul>
<p>If you like this sort of thing, here's a link to my author page which will bring you to my other works: <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/spikebrennan-s-personnel-file">http://www.scp-wiki.net/spikebrennan-s-personnel-file</a></p>

